= Discourse (linguistics) =

== Overview ==
In linguistics and related fields, '''discourse''' is contextualized language use beyond the analysis of isolated expressions.

Discourse can be spoken, signed, written, multimodal, conversational, institutional, narrative, argumentative, instructional, or digital.

The term names a broad area rather than one universally agreed unit or method.

== Beyond the sentence ==
Sentence grammar remains important, but connected communication contains organization that cannot be described by isolated sentence structure alone.

Discourse analysis can study:

* coherence and cohesion;
* topic and information structure;
* reference across utterances;
* turn-taking;
* repair;
* narrative organization;
* rhetorical relations;
* genre;
* stance and interaction;
* social roles and power.

== Context ==
[[Context (linguistics)|Context]] is central to discourse because interpretation depends on what came before, participant roles, goals, setting, and shared knowledge.

[[Common ground]] can evolve as a conversation progresses.

Discourse therefore provides the larger environment in which [[Presupposition|presuppositions]], [[Implicature|implicatures]], [[Speech act|speech acts]], reference, and anaphora operate.

== Discourse and text ==
A written text can be discourse, but discourse is not limited to text.

Conversation, signed interaction, spoken narrative, interviews, meetings, and multimodal exchanges are also discourse.

Likewise, not every sequence of sentences forms a coherent discourse merely because the sentences are adjacent.

== Approaches ==
Discourse studies includes several traditions:

* discourse analysis;
* conversation analysis;
* interactional sociolinguistics;
* critical discourse analysis;
* corpus discourse studies;
* formal discourse semantics;
* computational discourse analysis.

Their questions, evidence, and assumptions differ.

== Computational discourse ==
Natural-language processing studies discourse phenomena such as coreference, discourse relations, coherence, dialogue state, summarization, argument structure, and conversational modeling.

A model that processes long text is not automatically a discourse theory. Computational representations must state what discourse properties they encode.

